{0 Buses}

Buses carry typed messages between systems without either side knowing
about the other's private state. The default stack exposes three —
{!Eon_ecs.Signals}, {!Eon_ecs.Events}, {!Eon_ecs.Commands} — all satisfying
the same {!Eon_ecs.Bus.S} signature, differing only in {b when} an emitted
message becomes visible to subscribers. Examples below are from the
companion
{{:https://github.com/imunitic/eon/blob/main/eon_ecs/examples/book/buses_example.ml}[buses_example.ml]}.

{1 The shared shape}

{[
  type 'msg t
  val create  : unit -> 'msg t
  val on      : 'msg t -> ('msg -> unit) -> unit
  val clear   : 'msg t -> unit
  val emit    : 'msg t -> 'msg -> unit
  val collect : 'msg t -> unit
  val drain   : 'msg t -> unit
]}

[emit] only enqueues — nothing fires until [collect] or [drain] runs. The
buffering policy decides which of those, and when, actually delivers.

{1 Signals and Commands — same-frame delivery}

{!Eon_ecs.Signals} and {!Eon_ecs.Commands} are both backed by
{!Eon_ecs.Bus.Single} — [drain] and [collect] behave identically, and
whatever was [emit]ted since the last drain fires on the {b same} drain
call:

{[
  let bus    = Signals.create () in
  let fired  = ref false in
  Signals.on bus (fun `Damage_taken -> fired := true);

  Signals.emit bus `Damage_taken;
  assert (not !fired);   (* not delivered yet — emit only enqueues *)
  Signals.drain bus;
  assert !fired           (* delivered: drain ran in the same "frame" as emit *)
]}

The two are the same implementation under different names — the split is
purely a naming convention for intent. Use {b Signals} for fan-out
notifications where any number of systems may react (a hit landed, a level
loaded). Use {b Commands} when a system already knows exactly what mutation
to apply and is just describing it:

{[
  let bus   = Commands.create () in
  let total = ref 0 in
  Commands.on bus (fun (`Add_gold n) -> total := !total + n);

  Commands.emit bus (`Add_gold 10);
  Commands.emit bus (`Add_gold 5);
  Commands.drain bus;
  assert (!total = 15)
]}

{1 Events — next-frame delivery}

{!Eon_ecs.Events} is backed by {!Eon_ecs.Bus.Double}: [emit] writes into a
{b next} queue rather than the one about to be drained. [drain] delivers
whatever is in [current], then swaps [next] into [current]. An event
emitted during frame [N] is not visible until frame [N+1]'s drain:

{[
  let bus   = Events.create () in
  let fired = ref false in
  Events.on bus (fun `Level_up -> fired := true);

  Events.emit bus `Level_up;
  Events.drain bus;
  assert (not !fired);    (* frame N's drain: current was still empty *)

  Events.drain bus;
  assert !fired             (* frame N+1's drain: next -> current swap landed *)
]}

Use Events when a reaction genuinely needs to see "yesterday's" state — the
double-buffer swap is what makes that deferral deterministic instead of
depending on registration order within the same frame.

{1 Subscriber order}

Multiple handlers on the same bus fire in {b registration order} — the
first subscriber registered runs first:

{[
  let bus   = Signals.create () in
  let order = ref [] in
  Signals.on bus (fun `Ping -> order := !order @ [ 1 ]);   (* registered first *)
  Signals.on bus (fun `Ping -> order := !order @ [ 2 ]);   (* registered second *)
  Signals.emit bus `Ping;
  Signals.drain bus;
  assert (!order = [ 1; 2 ])   (* handler 1 (registered first) ran first *)
]}

In the default pipeline wiring, a system's handler registration order
tracks its position in the phase graph, so this also governs the order
handlers dispatch relative to each other across systems, not just within
one [on] call site.

